<sn><md>### 1. Update the system

```bash
sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade -y
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 2. Install Apache

```bash
sudo apt install apache2 -y
```

Check:

```bash
http://localhost
```

Apache's default web directory:

```text
/var/www/html
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 3. Install PHP

```bash
sudo apt install php libapache2-mod-php php-cli php-mysql -y
```

Check the installed version:

```bash
php -v
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 4. Install MySQ

```bash
sudo apt install mysql-server mysql-client -y
```

Secure it:

Check:

```bash
sudo systemctl status mysql
```

Open the database shell:

Check the version to verify installation :

```bash
mysql --version
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### 5. Install useful PHP extensions

```bash
sudo apt install \
php-curl \
php-mbstring \
php-xml \
php-zip \
php-gd \
php-intl \
php-mysql -y
```

Restart Apache:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart apache2
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Test PHP

Create a test file:

```bash
sudo nano /var/www/html/index.php
```

Add:

```php
<!--?php

phpinfo();
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost/index.php
```
--></md></sn><sn><md>### Better setup for development

Instead of putting projects directly in `/var/www/html`, create a workspace:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/Projects/php
```

Example:

```bash
mkdir ~/Projects/php/test
cd ~/Projects/php/test
```

Create:

```bash
nano index.php
```

```php
<!--?php

echo "Hello from PHP";
```

For simple development, PHP has its own built-in server:

```bash
php -S localhost:8000
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost:8000
```

This is often easier than configuring Apache for every small project.
--></md></sn><sn><md>### Recommended setup for you

Since you work on PHP projects such as **QDoc**, I recommend:

```text
~/Projects/
├── QDoc/
├── test-project/
└── other-project/
```

Then install:

```text
PHP
Composer
MariaDB
Apache or Nginx
phpMyAdmin (optional)
```

Install Composer:

```bash
sudo apt install composer -y
```

Check:

```bash
composer --version
```

For most modern PHP development, I would use **PHP + Composer + MariaDB**, and use either PHP's built-in server for development or Apache/Nginx when you need production-like routing and virtual hosts.
</md></sn>